## 5. Risk factors

1. **Competitive threat from soda music**: ByteDance’s soda music MAU has reached 130 million. Backed by Douyin’s huge traffic pool, it has changed the way music is discovered through the algorithm recommendation model. If TME cannot effectively respond to the rise of "recommended" music consumption, it may face continued diversion of user duration and new users.

2. **Structural decline of the social entertainment business**: This business revenue has declined for several consecutive quarters (-7.3% for the whole year of 2025, -11% for Q1 2026), and currently still accounts for 18.8% of revenue. The live streaming tipping model may continue to shrink due to tightening regulations and changes in user habits, dragging down overall growth.

3. **Policy Risks of Chinese Concept Stock ADRs**: Uncertainty in Sino-US relations, HFCAA audit requirements, and potential ADR forced delisting risks may lead to continued valuation discounts or even liquidity shocks. Although TME has been dual-listed in Hong Kong (01698.HK), ADR risk is still the core reason why institutional investors avoid it.

4. **Subscription growth slows down**: Q1 2026 subscription revenue growth dropped to 7% (previously 13%), and paid user growth dropped to 5.3%. The penetration rate of paid music in the Chinese market has reached a high level, and further growth may slow down.

5. **The risk of AI disrupting the music industry**: The rapid development of AI-generated music technology may change the way music content is produced and distributed. If AI music reduces the value of copyrighted content, TME’s bargaining power as a distribution platform may be weakened.

